WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.770
Spider-Man: Homecoming has arrived, and it's
far more tangled in the massive web of the

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:09.940
Marvel Cinematic Universe than anyone ever
expected, with references to over fifty years

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:10.760
of Marvel comics.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:16.710
If the average spider can lay anywhere between
ten and 1500 eggs, just how many Easter eggs

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.360
did Homecoming leave behind?

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.940
And if your Spidey-sense is tingling, that's
only because there are spoilers ahead.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:24.800
Damage Control

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.820
So, who cleans up after superheroes and their
arch-villains have their mega-destructive

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.320
throwdowns all over the globe?

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:36.800
In the comics, that job goes to Damage Control,
a government agency that's been around since

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:37.720
1988.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:43.060
Marvel's been kicking around the idea of a
Damage Control sitcom since 2015, but the

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.200
project never really made it off the ground.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.960
So, while we may never see a Marvel sitcom
a la DC's failed Powerless, Damage Control

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.760
has officially made it into continuity…
and Adrian Toomes is not happy.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.520
Betty Brant

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.760
Peter Parker's school has its own TV news
program, starring none other than Marvel mainstay

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:03.720
Betty Brant.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:10.720
Betty first appeared in The Amazing Spider-Man
#4, way back in 1963, as newspaper mogul J.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.380
Jonah Jameson's secretary.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.440
Eventually, she becomes a pretty successful
investigative reporter, and even has a fling

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:18.470
with the Hobgoblin.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:23.020
She plays a pretty big part in Spider-Man's
life, and a version of her even appeared in

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:27.770
Tobey Maguire's Spider-Man, so it's not unlikely
that she'll figure into the future, in one

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:28.540
way or another.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.120
Spider-Signal

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:34.760
We saw it in Civil War's post-credits scene,
but we see it again when Peter's tinkering

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.320
with his suit's devices in Homecoming.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.700
Comic fans all felt a weird tingle when Peter
briefly projected a Spider-Man logo onto the

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:47.700
wall, bringing back comic memories of Spider-Man's
most hilariously useless device.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.020
Contrary to Batman's awesome Bat-signal, which
summons the Dark Knight from great distances,

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.500
Spider-Man's Spider-Signal doesn't do much
more than announce his arrival to criminals,

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.600
and occasionally act as a flashlight.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.280
Not every idea from the early days of Spider-Man
was a good one.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:08.520
Those armpit-webs, on the other hand, will
always be pure comic book gold.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:10.340
Summon the Sinister Six

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:16.080
The appearance of Adrian Toomes, aka Vulture,
definitely isn't an Easter egg, but it probably

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.180
takes a real comic fan to spot some of Spidey's
other legendary foes.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:24.950
While the Sinister Six has taken many forms
over the years, the elderly Vulture has usually

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:26.020
been a core member.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:31.780
Also pretty prominent in Homecoming is Shocker,
who seems to be whoever's wearing an alien-tech-enhanced

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:32.300
glove.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:37.060
But the reference to the comics' Shocker goes
twofold when those same guys are seen wearing

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:41.920
the same distinctive, yellow quilted fabric
that the comic villain wears.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.220
"Shockeeeeeeerrrrrrrr!"

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.220
"You can't escape me!"

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.720
You'll also find Mac Gargan, aka Scorpion,
played by Michael Mando.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:03:00.540
In contrast to Homecoming's Gargan, comic
book Gargan started out as an investigative

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:05.620
reporter whose life is messed up by the experimental
process that gives him a giant, mechanical

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:06.560
scorpion tail.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:11.520
If there was any doubt about these two Gargans
being related, there's also that giant Scorpion

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.320
tattoo on big-screen Gargan's neck, too.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:19.050
We also get a whole lot of Hobgoblin, and
sorry to say, it's Peter Parker's biggest

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.860
fan and best friend, Ned.

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:26.760
While his full name is never given, Ned's
last name has been given as 'Leeds' in a few

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:27.960
different publications.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.860
Ned Leeds becomes one of at least seven different
Hobgoblins after being brainwashed by the

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:36.300
original Hobgoblin, Roderick Kingsley, to
take his place.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.740
Let's say that things don't end well.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.780
The Howling Commando legacy

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:45.240
If you think you're seeing a familiar face
at Peter Parker's school, it's because actor

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.700
Kenneth Choi is playing his own grandson.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:53.200
Way back in Captain America: The First Avenger,
Choi played Jim Morita, a member of Cap's

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:58.370
Howling Commandos, a legendary group of Marvel
Comics soldiers originally operating under

00:03:58.370 --> 00:03:59.370
Nick Fury.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:04.290
Choi returned in Homecoming to play Principal
Morita, a guy obviously very proud of his

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:07.660
grandpa, given the photo of Jim that can be
spotted in his office.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:13.200
Morita Jr. Jr.'s love of history also possibly
explains his affection for those terrible

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.349
Captain America instructional videos.

00:04:15.349 --> 00:04:20.200
And speaking of cameos in photos around the
school, the Incredible Hulk himself, Bruce

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:24.600
Banner, appears in one of the many posters
of great scientists featured around the science

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:25.220
classroom.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.380
And who's that on the school mural?

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.000
Howard Stark himself.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.220
Spider-Man 2.0

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:36.140
Fans of Donald Glover know that he kind of
campaigned to play Spider-Man in a big-screen

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:37.940
adaptation of the comic books.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.440
"And then someone put a big picture of me
in the comments and was like, 'Donald Glover

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:42.080
could play Spider-Man!

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.120
He's nerdy!'"

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.260
His interest in the role prompted Spider-scribe
Brian Michael Bendis to create a new Spider-Man

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:51.220
inspired by Glover named Miles Morales.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:55.520
And while Glover never made it to the big
screen as Spidey, he did voice the character

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.120
of Miles Morales on the Ultimate Spider-Man
animated series in 2015.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:05.220
Glover returned to the Spidey-verse in Homecoming
as Aaron Davis, a career criminal with the

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.300
alias of "Prowler," according to Spider-Man's
files.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:13.340
Sure, Marvel's main Prowler is some guy named
Hobie, but in the Ultimate Universe, it's

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:14.700
a guy named Aaron Davis.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:15.760
His nephew?

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:20.920
A guy named Miles Morales, the very same nephew
that Prowler refers to during his parking

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.840
garage confrontation with Spider-Man.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:28.860
And if that wasn't confirmation enough, check
out his license plate: UCS-M01, which is short

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:31.160
for Ultimate Comics, Spider-Man #1.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:32.220
Karen

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:37.220
Once Spider-Man has accessed his suit's full
capabilities, he's treated to a very JARVIS-like

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:41.440
computer which is able to cater to his every
need, which he names Karen.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:47.280
Avengers fans know that within the MCU, JARVIS
is Tony Stark's personal computer, which then

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.800
merged with a robot body, becoming Vision.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:55.160
Along the way, JARVIS was always played by
Paul Bettany, even as the Infinity Stone-wielding

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:55.680
Vision.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.980
In real life, Bettany is married to Jennifer
Connelly, who plays the voice of Karen in

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:04.480
Homecoming, keeping the pair of companion
computer programs as close as the people who

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:05.600
actually play them.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:07.280
True to the page

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:12.220
One of Spidey's most memorable scenes came
in Amazing Spider-Man #33.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:17.220
The comic's cover spells out Spidey's peril
pretty clearly, and it's nearly identical

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:21.620
to the scene in Homecoming when Vulture causes
the roof to cave in on Parker.

00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:26.180
For five pages, Spider-Man struggles under
wet rubble, thinking of his responsibilities

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:29.780
as the wall-crawler… and that gives him
the strength to set himself free.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:32.240
It's pretty much comic book perfect.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:33.620
Credits due

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:38.620
Blink and you'll miss it among the countless
scrawls of graffiti decorating Peter Parker's

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:43.320
New York City, but eagle-eyed viewers have
spotted the word 'Bagley' emblazoned amid

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:44.260
the colorful chaos.

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:49.920
Mark Bagley is noted in the Marvel Comics
universe for being a huge contributor to Spider-Man,

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:54.980
co-creating the villain Carnage, and illustrating
Venom's early turn as an anti-hero in the

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:58.920
Venom: Lethal Protector miniseries, so his
quick mention is no mistake.

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:04.200
And casual comic fans might be left wondering
why Jack Kirby is given any credit at all

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:09.621
for Spider-Man during the film's credits sequence,
since Spidey is one of the few 1960s comic

00:07:09.621 --> 00:07:12.380
characters that Kirby was never really associated
with.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:17.080
Comics history reveals that Kirby and Lee
sat down together to come up with Spider-Man,

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:21.919
and Kirby even drew the first batch of pages
for the hero, using his own designs, before

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:25.340
Lee rejected the concept and changed it a
bit to fit his own vision.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:31.259
Kirby ended up pencilling just the covers
of Spidey's debut in Amazing Fantasy #15 as

00:07:31.259 --> 00:07:36.800
well as Amazing Spider-Man #1, before moving
on to other projects, but his impact on Spider-Man

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:39.420
being acknowledged by Marvel is a pretty big
deal.

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:40.940
Thanks for watching!

00:07:40.949 --> 00:07:43.979
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:43.979 --> 00:07:46.210
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

